rd yeah i think so i think the  consumer's been in control for a long  time if you go back 20 years you look at  the fortune 500 top companies only half  of those actually exist and it's because  they failed to respond to their customer  fast enough they failed to reinvent  their services or their offering and  other disruptors have come in and taken  their place and so  you know it's it's the core of what we  do at aws every day is very much focused  on the customer obsessing about their  needs listening to them making sure we  can look round corners so we can predict  what they might want for the future  and how does this fit in with health and  fitness for you sammy  again i think it comes down to the the  mission of what you're trying to achieve  i think look most people  they want to improve their bodies they  want to improve their minds and it's  really really hard there are lots and  lots of quick fixes out there and we've  all seen it six week abs eight week abs  two week abs drink this tea you're gonna  feel great  the reality is that  they're all they're lies essentially i  mean it's you're trying to get someone  to buy a product once then buy it again  and we all know that fitness takes time  it takes commitment and it's hard and  you know fit we're trying to solve a  problem which is to make it easier for  people we're trying to make people build  healthy habits and we're trying to make  fitness part of their lifestyle and to  do that you've got to you've got to be  truthful you've got to take people on  that journey you can't just say look  you're going to get there in six weeks  there's six key levers that at any time  the businesses need to be pulling on and  they go from experience to marketing to  sales to the service you provide pricing  of course will be there and innovation  and it's really understanding at any one  time which levers to use in organization  be that some of the stuff that sam is  saying with fit do you need to pull on  different e